\", \"ordinary_weight\": 26, \"poincare_twist\": 21}, \"theorem_statement\": \"Let k be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, C a smooth projective connected curve of genus 6 over k, and gamma: C -> P^1 a finite flat morphism of degree four, with gamma_*O_C = O_{P^1} + E^vee (E of rank three and degree nine), Casnati-Ekedahl embedding i: C -> X = P(E^vee), p: X -> P^1, p_*O_X(1) = E. Assume (CE) the ideal of i has the standard degree-four Casnati-Ekedahl resolution 0 -> O_X(-4) tensor p^*det F -> O_X(-2) tensor p^*F -> I_C -> 0 with F of rank two; (N) O_X(1)|_C is isomorphic to omega_C tensor gamma^*O_{P^1}(2); and (BAL) F is isomorphic to O_{P^1}(4) + O_{P^1}(5). Put D = gamma^*O_{P^1}(1) and L = W_5 tensor omega_C^{-1} = omega_C(-D), of degree six with h^0(L) = 3. THEN: (A) L is base-point-free, so phi_L: C -> P^2 is a finite morphism onto an irreducible nondegenerate plane curve X_L with deg(phi_L)*deg(X_L) = 6. (B) Exactly one of two cases holds. Case (i): deg(phi_L) = 1 and X_L is a plane sextic with normalization C. Case (ii): deg(phi_L) = 2 and X_L is a smooth plane cubic; writing Y = X_L and psi = phi_L: C -> Y, the curve Y is elliptic, psi is a bielliptic double cover with psi_*O_C = O_Y + M^{-1}, deg M = 5, omega_C = psi^*M, L = psi^*N with N = O_{X_L}(1) of degree three, E is isomorphic to O(2)+O(3)+O(4), and gamma = q o psi where q = phi_{|P|}: Y -> P^1 is the degree-two morphism attached to P = M tensor N^{-1} in Pic^2(Y); in particular gamma is non-primitive and factors through a curve of genus one. The remaining possibilities do not occur: deg(phi_L) is never 3 or 6, X_L is never a singular cubic, C is never hyperelliptic and never a smooth plane quintic, and L is never the square of a base-point-free degree-three pencil. (C) In case (i), let |L|^{rs} be the open subset of |L| = P^2 of divisors with six pairwise-distinct points and Ord(|L|^{rs}) -> |L|^{rs} the finite etale right S_6-torsor of orderings. Then Ord(|L|^{rs}) is connected and the geometric monodromy image of pi_1^{et}(|L|^{rs}) -> S_6 is the full symmetric group S_6. (D) In case (ii), the fibres of psi cut each divisor of |L|^{rs} into three blocks of size two, and this partition is monodromy-invariant; hence the monodromy image lies in the imprimitive wreath subgroup S_2 wr S_3 of order 48 and is not S_6. (E) Case (ii) is nonempty. Let Y be an elliptic curve over k, P in Pic^2(Y) with degree-two morphism q = phi_{|P|}: Y -> P^1, M in Pic^5(Y) such that the unique point y_0 with O_Y(y_0) isomorphic to M tensor P^{-2} is not a ramification point of q, and B in |M^{tensor 2}| reduced; let psi: C -> Y be the double cover branched along B. Then C is smooth connected of genus six, gamma = q o psi is finite flat of degree four, and (C,gamma) satisfies (BAL). Such data exist, and (CE) and (N) hold for every such (C,gamma) by the source-backed input SRC-IN-1. Hence the balanced locus contains tetragonal pencils factoring through a genus-one intermediate curve. (F) Let S be a reduced locally Noetherian k-scheme carrying a genus-six degree-four family satisfying (CE), (BAL) and (N) on every geometric fibre, AJ = P_S(pi_*L), U the reduced-support open of AJ, and Z = Ord(U) -> U the finite etale S_6-torsor of orderings of ATT-0088 (III). If S_alpha is a connected component of S over which some geometric fibre is of type (i), then the geometric monodromy image H_alpha of pi_1^{et}(U_alpha) -> S_6 equals S_6; consequently Z_alpha is connected, and if U_alpha is in addition normal, integral and locally Noetherian then Z_alpha is irreducible, the component index [S_6 : H_alpha] equals one, and the S_6-induction functor of FND-0152 is the identity. No Hodge, weight or Tate conclusion is asserted anywhere in (A)-(F).\", \"theorem_scope\": {\"g\": 6, \"n\": 6, \"base\": \"Parts (A)-(E) are statements about a single geometric point, i.e. a pair (C,gamma) over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. Part (F) is stated over a reduced locally Noetherian k-scheme S, exactly the scope of ATT-0088 Part III; algebraic-stack bases are not covered.\", \"hypotheses\": \"(CE) the standard degree-four Casnati-Ekedahl ideal resolution; (N) the relative-canonical normalization O_X(1)|_C = omega_C tensor gamma^*O(2) (equivalently the weak normalization of FND-0105 with residual twist A trivial, as recorded in ATT-0088/CLM-0088-1); (BAL) F = O(4)+O(5). (CE) and (N) are carried as explicit hypotheses in (A)-(D) and (F) and are never asserted as proved here; in (E) they are supplied for the constructed cover by the source-backed input SRC-IN-1 (Casnati-Ekedahl 1996 Theorem 2.1 plus SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6), in exactly the manner prescribed by FND-0150.\", \"generality\": \"The dichotomy (B), the base-point-freeness (A), the trigonal exclusion, and the plane-quintic exclusion are specific to (g, deg L) = (6,6) and to degree-four gamma; the monodromy argument in (C) uses only that phi_L is a birational morphism from a smooth curve onto an irreducible nondegenerate plane curve of degree six over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. The existence statement (E) holds for every admissible (Y,P,M,B) as stated, not merely for one example.\", \"not_covered\": \"the exact monodromy image in case (ii); whether a connected component of the balanced base can consist entirely of type-(ii) fibres; non-primitive degree-four pencils factoring through intermediate curves of genus two or three; stabilizers Aut(C,Gamma) and Aut(C,gamma,Gamma) and any inertia or full-faithfulness statement; the existence, faithfulness, quasi-finiteness, closedness, image closure or absolute codimension of a morphism to M_{6,6}; closedness, purity, lci or Gysin statements for the pair (M_{6,6},Z); nonreduced bases and algebraic-stack bases; irreducibility of the balanced base itself; unbalanced CE strata; integral coefficients and coarse-space statements; and every Hodge, weight, purity and Tate-type conclusion.\"}, \"summary\": \"Determines the finite-cover behaviour of the ordered S_6-torsor over the balanced tetragonal CE geometry at (g,n)=(6,6), and settles two of the recorded frontier obligations. Working on a single balanced geometric fibre under (CE)+(N)+(BAL), the residual bundle L = omega_C(-D) is shown to be base-point-free (a point ATT-0088/REV-0142 explicitly leave open), and the associated degree-six morphism phi_L: C -> P^2 is shown to be either birational onto a plane sextic (type (i)) or exactly two-to-one onto a smooth plane cubic, in which case gamma is bielliptic-composed (type (ii)). The intermediate possibilities are eliminated: hyperelliptic fibres by the confirmed CLM-0088-2, smooth plane quintics and the trigonal case L = T^{tensor 2} by two new section-count computations of the same kind as the confirmed hyperelliptic obstruction. In type (i) the fibrewise geometric monodromy of the S_6-ordering torsor is proved to be the full symmetric group, by combining transitivity and two-transitivity of the incidence varieties with a transposition supplied by a general tangent line and Jordan's theorem; this is the first determination of a monodromy image for this cover and it needs no irreducibility hypothesis on the balanced base. In type (ii) the monodromy is imprimitive, contained in S_2 wr S_3. Type (ii) is then shown to be nonempty by an explicit but universal construction: for every elliptic Y, every P in Pic^2(Y) and every M in Pic^5(Y) whose associated point y_0 avoids the four ramification points of q = phi_{|P|}, the double cover of Y branched along a reduced member of |M^{tensor 2}| is a smooth genus-six curve whose composed degree-four pencil is balanced. Hence a non-primitive degree-four pencil through a positive-genus intermediate curve can have balanced CE splitting, answering the corresponding frontier obligation affirmatively for intermediate genus one, and showing that primitivity of gamma is not available on the balanced locus. The family consequence is stated component-locally: over any connected component of a reduced base meeting the type-(i) locus, the monodromy image is S_6, the ordering torsor is connected, and (under normality) irreducible, so the FND-0152 induction is trivial. No Tate-type conclusion is drawn.\", \"argument_markdown\": \"## 0. Conventions, standing hypotheses, and inputs used\\n\\nThroughout, `k` is an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. `C` is a smooth projective connected curve of genus `g = 6` over `k`, and `gamma: C -> P^1` is a nonconstant morphism of degree four; since `C` and `P^1` are smooth curves, `gamma` is automatically finite and flat, and `C` is Gorenstein.\\n\\nWrite `gamma_*O_C = O_{P^1} (+) E^vee` with `E` locally free of rank three (the Tschirnhausen data), `X := P(E^vee)` with structure map `p: X -> P^1` normalized by `p_*O_X(1) = E`, and `i: C -> X` the relative-canonical (Casnati-Ekedahl) embedding.\\n\\n**Standing hypotheses.**\\n\\n- **(CE)** The ideal of `i` has the standard degree-four Casnati-Ekedahl resolution\\n  `0 -> O_X(-4) (x) p^*det F -> O_X(-2) (x) p^*F -> I_C -> 0`, with `F` locally free of rank two on `P^1`.\\n- **(N)** `O_X(1)|_C ~= omega_C (x) gamma^*O_{P^1}(2)`.\\n- **(BAL)** `F ~= O_{P^1}(4) (+) O_{P^1}(5)`.\\n\\nThese are exactly the hypotheses `(CE)`, `(N)`, `(BAL)` of ATT-0088 (dual-confirmed by REV-0139 and REV-0142), with `(N)` taken in the form with trivial residual twist. That normalization is legitimate: ATT-0088/CLM-0088-1 proves, from the weak form `O_X(1)|_C ~= omega_{C/P^1} (x) gamma^*A`, that `deg A = 0` and hence `A ~= O_{P^1}`. I do **not** assert `(CE)` or `(N)`; they are carried as hypotheses in Parts (A)-(D) and (F). In Part (E) they are discharged for the constructed cover by a cited source (SRC-IN-1 below), which is exactly the discharge route prescribed by packet finding FND-0150.\\n\\nSet\\n\\n- `D := gamma^*O_{P^1}(1)` (an effective, base-point-free divisor class of degree four),\\n- `W_5 := (O_X(2) (x) p^*O_{P^1}(-5))|_C` in the packet convention, and `L := W_5 (x) omega_C^{-1}`,\\n- `R := O_X(2)|_C`.\\n\\n### 0.1 Verified inputs quoted from dependency artifacts\\n\\nThe following are used as verified inputs, with artifact identifiers, not reproved:\\n\\n- **[V1]** ATT-0088/CLM-0088-1 (confirmed by REV-0139 and REV-0142): under (CE)+(BAL)+(N), on every geometric fibre `A` is trivial, `W_5 = omega_C^{(x)2}(-D)`, `L = omega_C(-D)`, `deg W_4 = 20`, `deg W_5 = 16`, `deg L = 6`, `chi(L) = 1`.\\n- **[V2]** ATT-0088/CLM-0088-2 (confirmed by REV-0139 and REV-0142): under (CE)+(BAL) alone, **no balanced geometric fibre is hyperelliptic**. (Independently recorded as packet FND-0125 and as an established working-context fact; REV-0135 and REV-0138 re-derived the underlying section count `h^0(F(-6)) >= 9 - 7 = 2` for `E = O(1)+O(4)^2` end to end.)\\n- **[V3]** ATT-0088/CLM-0088-3 (confirmed by REV-0139 and REV-0142): under (CE)+(BAL)+(N), on every geometric fibre `h^0(O_C(D)) = 2`, `h^0(L) = 3`, `h^1(L) = 2`, so `|L| ~= P^2`; moreover **every summand of `E` has degree at least two**.\\n- **[V4]** ATT-0088/CLM-0088-5 (confirmed by REV-0139 and REV-0142): on every balanced geometric fibre satisfying (N), `|L|` contains a divisor of six pairwise-distinct points; the non-reduced locus is closed of dimension exactly one; the reduced-support open `|L|^{rs}` is nonempty, dense, of dimension two and not closed in `|L|`; and the ordered incidence in `Conf_6(C)` is nonempty, smooth of pure dimension two, and a **finite etale `S_6`-torsor of degree 720** over `|L|^{rs}`.\\n- **[V5]** ATT-0088/CLM-0088-8 (confirmed by REV-0139 and REV-0142): over a reduced locally Noetherian base `S`, `pi_*L` is locally free of rank three, `AJ = P_S(pi_*L)` is a Zariski-locally trivial `P^2`-bundle, its reduced-support open `U` is `S`-smooth of pure relative dimension two and surjective onto `S`, and `Z` is canonically the `S_6`-torsor of orderings of the universal divisor over `U`.\\n- **[V6]** ATT-0086/CLM-0086-1 and CLM-0086-2 (confirmed by REV-0135 and REV-0138), and ATT-0066/CLM-0066-3 (confirmed by REV-0110 and REV-0116): the jump stratum `B_4 = {h^0(L) = 4}` is empty on the balanced locus, so `h^0(L) = 3` identically. (Also recorded as packet FND-0139.)\\n- **[V7]** ATT-0065/CLM-0065-9 (confirmed by REV-0108 and REV-0109): at balanced `(g,n) = (6,6)` the ambient CE evaluation-failure scheme equals `a_N^{-1}(e)`, i.e. the relative incidence `O_C(Gamma) ~= L`, scheme-theoretically and after arbitrary base change. This is what makes `L` the correct object of study; it is not otherwise used in the proofs below.\\n- **[SRC-IN-1]** *Source-backed input, not a proved step.* For every finite flat Gorenstein cover of degree `d >= 3`, Casnati-Ekedahl 1996, Theorem 2.1 supplies the relative-canonical embedding and the Casnati-Ekedahl resolution, which for `d = 4` is the two-term resolution in (CE); and SRC-0004 (Canning-Larson, Lemma 10.6, quoted in the packet's primary locator excerpts) records `O_{P(E^vee)}(1)|_C ~= omega_C (x) gamma^*omega_{P^1}^vee = omega_C (x) gamma^*O(2)`, i.e. (N) with trivial twist. Packet finding FND-0150 records precisely this pair of citations as the sanctioned way to discharge (CE)+(N) for a concrete smooth degree-four cover. I use SRC-IN-1 only in Part (E), and only to say that the constructed cover satisfies (CE)+(N); the mathematical content of (E), namely that (BAL) holds, is proved from (CE)+(N) here.\\n\\n### 0.2 Numerology\\n\\nAll of the following are elementary consequences of the setup and agree with the pinned reusable computations in the working context.\\n\\n- `chi(gamma_*O_C) = chi(O_C) = 1 - g = -5` and `rk(gamma_*O_C) = 4`, so `deg(gamma_*O_C) = -5 - 4 = -9`, i.e. `deg E^vee = -9` and `deg E = 9`.\\n- Under (N), `R = O_X(2)|_C = omega_C^{(x)2} (x) gamma^*O(4)`, so `deg R = 2*10 + 4*4 = 36`. (This reproduces `deg O_{P(E^vee)}(2)|_C = 2(2g-2+4*2) = 36`.)\\n- `deg W_f|_C = 2*18 - 4f = 4g + 12 - 4f`, hence `deg W_5 = 16`, `deg W_4 = 20`, and `deg L = 16 - 10 = 6`.\\n- `L^{(x)2} = omega_C^{(x)2}(-2D) = R (x) gamma^*O(-6)`, of degree `36 - 24 = 12 > 2g - 2 = 10`; therefore `h^1(L^{(x)2}) = 0` and `h^0(L^{(x)2}) = 12 - 6 + 1 = 7`.\\n\\n## 1. The balancedness criterion\\n\\n**Lemma 1.** Assume (CE) and (N). Write `gamma_*O_C = O (+) (+)_{i=1}^{3} O(-a_i)` with `a_1 <= a_2 <= a_3`, so `E = (+)_i O(a_i)` and `sum_i a_i = 9`. Then:\\n\\n1. `h^0(C, gamma^*O(m)) = (m+1)_+ + sum_i (m + 1 - a_i)_+` for all `m` in `Z`.\\n2. `F = p_*(I_C(2))` and there is a short exact sequence `0 -> F -> Sym^2 E -> gamma_*R -> 0` on `P^1`; `F` has rank two and degree nine.\\n3. Fix a splitting `E = (+)_i O(a_i)` with generators `x_i`. Under (N), `gamma_*(omega_C (x) gamma^*O(2)) = O (+) E`, and the CE inclusion `E -> gamma_*(O_X(1)|_C)` is the inclusion of that summand; let `xi_i` in `H^0(C, omega_C (x) gamma^*O(2 - a_i))` be the section corresponding to `x_i`. Then the restriction map\\n   `res: H^0(P^1, Sym^2E(-6)) -> H^0(C, L^{(x)2})`\\n   sends `sum_{i<=j} c_{ij} x_i x_j` (with `c_{ij}` in `H^0(O_{P^1}(a_i + a_j - 6))`) to `sum_{i<=j} gamma^*(c_{ij}) * xi_i xi_j`, and `ker(res) = H^0(P^1, F(-6))`.\\n4. Writing `F ~= O(s) (+) O(9-s)` with `s <= 4`, one has `h^0(F(-6)) = 4 - s`. Consequently **(BAL) holds if and only if `res` is injective**; and if `res` fails to be injective then (BAL) fails.\\n\\n*Proof.* (1) `h^0(C, gamma^*O(m)) = h^0(P^1, gamma_*O_C (x) O(m))` by finiteness of `gamma`, and the displayed formula is the sum of `h^0` over the summands.\\n\\n(2) Twist the resolution (CE) by `O_X(2)`: `0 -> p^*(det F)(-2) -> p^*F -> I_C(2) -> 0`. Since `p` is a `P^2`-bundle, `R^j p_*O_X(-2) = 0` for all `j` (the only nonvanishing higher direct image of `O_X(t)` for `t < 0` occurs at `t <= -3`). Hence `p_*(p^*(det F)(-2)) = 0` and `R^1 p_*(p^*(det F)(-2)) = 0`, while `R^1 p_*(p^*F) = 0`, so `p_*(I_C(2)) = F` and `R^1 p_*(I_C(2)) = 0`. Pushing `0 -> I_C(2) -> O_X(2) -> O_C(2) -> 0` along `p` and using `p_*O_X(2) = Sym^2E`, `p_*O_C(2) = gamma_*R`, and the vanishing of `R^1p_*(I_C(2))`, gives the stated short exact sequence. Then `chi(Sym^2E) = 4*deg E + 6 = 42`, `chi(gamma_*R) = chi(R) = 36 - 6 + 1 = 31`, so `chi(F) = 11 = deg F + 2` and `deg F = 9`.\\n\\n(3) By relative duality `gamma_*omega_C = Hom(gamma_*O_C, omega_{P^1}) = omega_{P^1} (+) (E (x) omega_{P^1})`, so `gamma_*(omega_C (x) gamma^*O(2)) = O (+) E`. Under (N) this is `gamma_*(O_X(1)|_C)`, and in the Casnati-Ekedahl construction the embedding `i` is the one induced by the surjection `gamma^*E -> omega_{C/P^1} = O_X(1)|_C` adjoint to the inclusion of the summand `E`. The map `Sym^2E -> gamma_*R` of (2) is `p_*` of `O_X(2) -> O_X(2)|_C`, hence on the chosen generators it is multiplication of the corresponding sections. Twisting by `O(-6)` and taking `H^0` of the left-exact sequence in (2) gives `ker(res) = H^0(F(-6))`. Note `R (x) gamma^*O(-6) = omega_C^{(x)2}(-2D) = L^{(x)2}`, so the target is `H^0(C, L^{(x)2})`, of dimension `7` by 0.2.\\n\\n(4) `F(-6) = O(s-6) (+) O(3-s)` with `s <= 4`, so `h^0(F(-6)) = 0 + (4-s) = 4-s`. This vanishes exactly when `s = 4`, i.e. exactly under (BAL). Combined with `ker(res) = H^0(F(-6))` this is the stated criterion. QED\\n\\nLemma 1(4) is the engine of the whole argument, and it is the same criterion the two confirmation reviews REV-0135 and REV-0138 re-derived independently for the hyperelliptic case (`h^0(Sym^2E(-6)) = 9`, target of dimension `7`, hence `h^0(F(-6)) >= 2`). REV-0138 records explicitly that the target has `h^0 = 7` *whatever* degree-12 line bundle it is, which is the normalization-free form used there. Here I keep (N), so the target is literally `H^0(L^{(x)2})`.\\n\\n## 2. Two exclusions by section count\\n\\n### 2.1 Smooth plane quintics\\n\\n**Lemma 2.** Let `C` be a smooth plane quintic of genus six (so `omega_C = O_C(2H)` with `H` the hyperplane class), and let `gamma: C -> P^1` be any degree-four morphism. Then, assuming (CE) and (N), `F` is not balanced.\\n\\n*Proof.* *Step 1: `C` is not hyperelliptic.* `H` is special, since `h^1(H) = h^0(2H - H) = h^0(H) = 3 > 0`. On a hyperelliptic curve every special complete system is `r` copies of the `g^1_2` plus base points; with `h^0(H) = 3` this forces `r = 2` and `deg H = 5` gives a base point, contradicting very ampleness of `H`.\\n\\n*Step 2: `C` is not trigonal.* Suppose `T` is a `g^1_3`; it is base-point-free (a base point would give a `g^1_2`, excluded by Step 1). The base-point-free pencil trick applied to `H^0(T) (x) H^0(H) -> H^0(T+H)` has kernel `H^0(H - T)`. If `h^0(H-T) = 0` the image has dimension at least `2*3 = 6`; but `deg(T+H) = 8`, `h^1(T+H) = h^0(2H - T - H) = h^0(H-T) = 0`, so `h^0(T+H) = 3 < 6`, a contradiction. Hence `H ~ T + q_1 + q_2` for an effective degree-two divisor. But `H` is very ample, so two points impose independent conditions on `|H|`, giving `h^0(H - q_1 - q_2) = 1`, contradicting `h^0(T) = 2`.\\n\\n*Step 3: every `g^1_4` on `C` is `|H - p_0|`.* Let `D` be a `g^1_4`. It is base-point-free (a base point would give a `g^1_3`, excluded by Step 2). Apply the base-point-free pencil trick to `H^0(D) (x) H^0(H) -> H^0(D+H)`; the kernel is `H^0(H-D)`, of degree one. If `h^0(H-D) = 0`, the image has dimension at least six; but `deg(D+H) = 9`, `h^1(D+H) = h^0(H-D) = 0`, so `h^0(D+H) = 4 < 6`, a contradiction. Hence `H - D ~ p_0` for a point `p_0`, i.e. `D ~ H - p_0`.\\n\\n*Step 4: the CE data.* With `D = H - p_0` one has `L = omega_C - D = 2H - H + p_0 = H + p_0`. Now `h^0(H+p_0)`: its degree is six, `chi = 1`, and `h^1(H+p_0) = h^0(2H - H - p_0) = h^0(H - p_0) = 2`, so `h^0(H+p_0) = 3 = h^0(H)`. Therefore **`p_0` is a base point of `|L|`**, and every section of `L` vanishes at `p_0`. (This reproduces the working-context established fact about the plane-quintic base point.)\\n\\nCompute `E` by Lemma 1(1). First `h^0(D) = h^0(H - p_0) = 2`, so `2 = 2 + sum_i (2 - a_i)_+` forces `a_i >= 2` for all `i` (this also agrees with [V3]). Next `h^0(2D) = h^0(2H - 2p_0) = h^0(omega_C - 2p_0) = 6 - 2 = 4`, using that `2p_0` imposes two independent conditions on the canonical system precisely because `C` is not hyperelliptic. So `4 = 3 + sum_i (3-a_i)_+`, i.e. `sum_i (3-a_i)_+ = 1`. With `a_i >= 2` and `sum a_i = 9`, the only solution is `(a_1,a_2,a_3) = (2,3,4)`, i.e. `E = O(2)+O(3)+O(4)`.\\n\\n*Step 5: the kernel.* `Sym^2E(-6)` has summands of degrees `-2, -1, 0, 0, 1, 2`, so `h^0(Sym^2E(-6)) = 0+0+1+1+2+3 = 7`. The section levels of Lemma 1(3) are\\n`xi_2` in `H^0(omega_C (x) gamma^*O(0)) = H^0(2H)`,\\n`xi_3` in `H^0(omega_C (x) gamma^*O(-1)) = H^0(2H - D) = H^0(H + p_0)`,\\n`xi_4` in `H^0(omega_C (x) gamma^*O(-2)) = H^0(2H - 2D) = H^0(2p_0)`.\\nSince `C` is not hyperelliptic, `h^0(2p_0) = 1` and the unique section has divisor exactly `2p_0`, so `ord_{p_0}(xi_4) = 2`. By Step 4, `ord_{p_0}(xi_3) >= 1`. Trivially `ord_{p_0}(xi_2) >= 0` and `ord_{p_0}(gamma^*c) >= 0` for any `c`.\\nThe monomials with `a_i + a_j >= 6` are exactly `x_2x_4`, `x_3^2`, `x_3x_4`, `x_4^2` (the coefficient spaces for `x_2^2` and `x_2x_3` are `H^0(O(-2))` and `H^0(O(-1))`, both zero). Their restrictions have vanishing order at `p_0` at least `0+2 = 2`, `1+1 = 2`, `1+2 = 3`, `2+2 = 4` respectively, hence at least `2` in every case. Therefore `image(res)` is contained in `H^0(L^{(x)2}(-2p_0)) = H^0(2H + 2p_0 - 2p_0) = H^0(2H)`, of dimension six. Since the source has dimension seven, `ker(res) != 0`, so `h^0(F(-6)) >= 1` and `F` is not balanced by Lemma 1(4). QED\\n\\n### 2.2 The trigonal case `L = T^{(x)2}`\\n\\n**Lemma 3.** Assume (CE), (N), (BAL). Then `L` is not isomorphic to `T^{(x)2}` for a base-point-free pencil `T` of degree three.\\n\\n*Proof.* Suppose `L = 2T` with `t: C -> P^1` the degree-three morphism attached to `T`. By [V2], `C` is not hyperelliptic; by [V3], `h^0(L) = 3` and `h^0(O_C(D)) = 2` with `D = gamma^*O(1)` base-point-free of degree four. Since `t^*: H^0(P^1, O(2)) -> H^0(2T)` is injective between spaces of dimension three, it is an isomorphism:\\n\\n  **(*) `H^0(L) = H^0(2T) = t^*H^0(P^1, O_{P^1}(2))`**, so every divisor of `|2T|` is `t^*(e)` for a unique `e` in `|O_{P^1}(2)|`.\\n\\n*Step 1: `h^0(4T - omega_C) = 0`.* Note `4T - omega_C = 2L - omega_C = omega_C - 2D` has degree `2`. Since `C` is not hyperelliptic, `h^0` of a degree-two bundle is at most one. Suppose `h^0(4T - omega_C) = 1`, so `4T ~ omega_C + A` with `A >= 0` of degree two. Then `D = omega_C - 2T ~ 2T - A`, and by (*),\\n`|D| = { t^*(e) - A : e in |O(2)|, t^*(e) >= A }`.\\nWrite `A = a_1 + a_2` (possibly `a_1 = a_2`).\\n - If `t(a_1) != t(a_2)`: then `e` must contain both points, so `e` is unique and `h^0(D) = 1`, contradicting `h^0(D) = 2`.\\n - If `a_1 != a_2` and `t(a_1) = t(a_2) = s`: then `t^*(e) >= A` iff `e >= s`, so `|D| = (t^*(s) - A) + |T|` and `t^*(s) - A` is a single point `p_0`, which is then a base point of `|D|`, contradicting base-point-freeness of `D`.\\n - If `a_1 = a_2 = a`, `s = t(a)`: if `t` is unramified at `a`, then `t^*(e) >= 2a` forces `e >= 2s`, so `e` is unique and `h^0(D) = 1`, a contradiction; if `t` is ramified at `a`, then `e >= s` suffices, `|D| = (t^*(s) - 2a) + |T|`, and `t^*(s) - 2a` is a single point, again a base point of `|D|`, a contradiction.\\nAll cases are impossible, so `h^0(4T - omega_C) = 0`.\\n\\n*Step 2: `E = O(3)^{(+)3}`.* By Lemma 1(1) and `h^0(D) = 2` we get `a_i >= 2` for all `i` (also [V3]). Next `h^0(2D) = chi(2D) + h^1(2D) = (8 - 6 + 1) + h^0(omega_C - 2D) = 3 + h^0(4T - omega_C) = 3` by Step 1. So `3 = 3 + sum_i (3 - a_i)_+`, forcing `a_i >= 3` for all `i`; with `sum a_i = 9` this gives `a_i = 3` for all `i`.\\n\\n*Step 3: the kernel.* `Sym^2E(-6) = O^{(+)6}`, so `h^0(Sym^2E(-6)) = 6` and every coefficient `c_{ij}` lies in `H^0(O(0))`, i.e. is a constant. Also `E(-3) = O^{(+)3}` sits inside `gamma_*(omega_C (x) gamma^*O(-1)) = gamma_*L`, and `h^0(E(-3)) = 3 = h^0(L)`, so the three sections `xi_i` form a basis of `H^0(L)`. Consequently `res` is exactly the multiplication map\\n`Sym^2 H^0(L) -> H^0(L^{(x)2})`.\\nBy (*), `H^0(L) = t^*H^0(O_{P^1}(2))`, hence the image of `res` lies in `t^*H^0(O_{P^1}(4))`, which has dimension five (as `t^*` is injective). The source has dimension six, so `ker(res) != 0`, `h^0(F(-6)) >= 1`, and `F` is not balanced by Lemma 1(4). This contradicts (BAL). QED\\n\\n*Remark.* The same conclusion is reached with no use of the Maroni/Hirzebruch classification of trigonal curves. Note also that `E = O(3)^{(+)3}` by itself is compatible with balancedness (the working context records a `(1,2)`-`(2,2)` complete intersection in `P^1 x P^2` with `E^vee = O(-3)^3` and `F = O(4)+O(5)`); what is incompatible is `E = O(3)^3` *together with* `H^0(L)` being a pullback along a degree-three map. So the exclusion is genuinely about `L = 2T`, not about the splitting type of `E`.\\n\\n## 3. Part (A): `L` is base-point-free\\n\\n**Proposition 4.** Assume (CE), (N), (BAL). Then `L` is base-point-free.\\n\\n*Proof.* By [V3] and [V6], `h^0(L) = 3` on every balanced fibre. Suppose `p` is a base point, i.e. `h^0(L(-p)) = 3`. Since `L = omega_C - D`, we have `L(-p) = omega_C - (D+p)`, and `chi(D+p) = 5 - 6 + 1 = 0`, so by Riemann-Roch and Serre duality `h^0(D+p) = h^1(D+p) = h^0(omega_C - D - p) = h^0(L(-p)) = 3`. Thus `|D+p|` is a `g^2_5`.\\n\\n- If `|D+p|` has a base point `q`, then `|D + p - q|` is a `g^2_4`, so `h^0 = 3 = deg/2 + 1`, i.e. equality in Clifford's theorem, with `deg = 4` not in `{0, 2g-2} = {0,10}`. Clifford equality then forces `C` hyperelliptic, contradicting [V2].\\n- If `|D+p|` is base-point-free, it defines a finite morphism `phi: C -> P^2` with nondegenerate image `X'` (the system is complete of projective dimension two), and `deg(phi) * deg(X') = 5`. Nondegeneracy gives `deg X' >= 2`, so `deg phi = 1` and `deg X' = 5`. Then `X'` is an irreducible plane quintic whose normalization is `C` of genus six; since the arithmetic genus of a plane quintic is `(5-1)(5-2)/2 = 6`, the delta invariant is zero and `X'` is smooth, so `C` is a smooth plane quintic. By Lemma 2 this contradicts (BAL).\\n\\nBoth branches are impossible, so `L` has no base point. QED\\n\\nSince `L` is base-point-free of degree six with `h^0(L) = 3`, the morphism `phi_L: C -> P^2 = P(H^0(L)^vee)` is finite (it is nonconstant, and `C` is a curve) onto an irreducible curve `X_L` which is nondegenerate because the system is complete; and `deg(phi_L) * deg(X_L) = deg L = 6`. This is Part (A). Under the identification of `|L|` with the dual plane `(P^2)^vee`, the members of `|L|` are exactly the pullbacks `phi_L^*(l)` of lines `l`.\\n\\n*Comparison with the dependency.* ATT-0088/CLM-0088-4 proves only that the fixed part of `|L|` has degree at most one and the moving part is base-point-free of degree **five or six**, and REV-0142 records explicitly that \\\"No claim is made that `L` itself is base-point-free\\\"; ATT-0088's `theorem_scope.not_covered` lists base-point-freeness as \\\"neither asserted nor denied\\\". Proposition 4 removes the degree-five alternative. (Packet finding FND-0134 asserts the same conclusion by the same plane-quintic route; the point of Lemma 2 and Proposition 4 is to supply the proof, including the missing step that every `g^1_4` on a plane quintic is `|H-p_0|`.)\\n\\n## 4. Part (B): the dichotomy\\n\\n**Proposition 5.** Assume (CE), (N), (BAL). Then either `deg phi_L = 1` (type (i)) or `deg phi_L = 2` with `X_L` a smooth plane cubic and `gamma` bielliptic-composed as in the theorem statement (type (ii)).\\n\\n*Proof.* `deg phi_L` divides `6` and `deg X_L = 6/deg phi_L >= 2`.\\n\\n- `deg phi_L = 6` would give `deg X_L = 1`, contradicting nondegeneracy.\\n- `deg phi_L = 3` gives `deg X_L = 2`; an irreducible nondegenerate plane conic is smooth, hence isomorphic to `P^1`. So `t := (C -> X_L ~= P^1)` is a degree-three morphism, and `L = phi_L^*O_{P^2}(1) = t^*(O_{P^1}(2)) = 2T` with `T := t^*O_{P^1}(1)` a base-point-free `g^1_3`. This is excluded by Lemma 3.\\n- `deg phi_L = 2` gives `deg X_L = 3`. Suppose `X_L` were singular. Then its normalization is `P^1` (an irreducible singular plane cubic has geometric genus zero), and since `C` is smooth, `phi_L` factors through the normalization, giving a degree-two morphism `h: C -> P^1`, i.e. `C` hyperelliptic, contradicting [V2]. (Alternatively and independently: then `L = h^*O_{P^1}(3)` would have `h^0 >= 4 > 3`.) So `X_L` is a smooth plane cubic, i.e. an elliptic curve `Y`, and `psi := phi_L : C -> Y` is a degree-two morphism of smooth curves, hence (char 0) a cyclic double cover with `psi_*O_C = O_Y (+) M^{-1}` for a line bundle `M` on `Y` with `M^{(x)2} = O_Y(B)`, `B` the branch divisor. Riemann-Hurwitz: `2*6 - 2 = 2(2*1-2) + deg B`, so `deg B = 10` and `deg M = 5`. Since `omega_Y = O_Y`, `omega_C = psi^*(omega_Y (x) M) = psi^*M`. Also `L = psi^*N` with `N := O_{X_L}(1)|_Y` of degree three.\\n  Then `D = omega_C - L = psi^*(M (x) N^{-1}) = psi^*P` with `P := M (x) N^{-1}` in `Pic^2(Y)`, and\\n  `h^0(psi^*P) = h^0(Y,P) + h^0(Y, P (x) M^{-1}) = 2 + 0 = 2`\\n  (the second term vanishes because `deg(P - M) = -3 < 0`). Since `psi^*H^0(P)` is a two-dimensional subspace of the two-dimensional `H^0(D)`, we get `|D| = psi^*|P|`, hence `gamma = q o psi` with `q = phi_{|P|}: Y -> P^1` of degree two (`P` has degree `2 >= 2g(Y)`, hence is base-point-free with `h^0 = 2`). So `gamma` is non-primitive, factoring through the genus-one curve `Y`.\\n  Finally, by Lemma 1(1), `h^0(mD) = h^0(Y, mP) + h^0(Y, mP - M)`. For `m = 1`: `2 + 0 = 2`, so `a_i >= 2` for all `i`. For `m = 2`: `h^0(2P) + h^0(2P - M) = 4 + 0 = 4`, so `sum_i(3 - a_i)_+ = 1`; with `a_i >= 2` and `sum a_i = 9` the unique solution is `(2,3,4)`, i.e. `E = O(2)+O(3)+O(4)`.\\n- `deg phi_L = 1` is type (i): `X_L` is a plane sextic and `phi_L` is its normalization, so `X_L` has geometric genus six and total delta invariant `10 - 6 = 4`.\\n\\nThe two cases are mutually exclusive since they are distinguished by `deg phi_L`. QED\\n\\n## 5. Part (C): full `S_6` monodromy in type (i)\\n\\nFix a type-(i) pair `(C,gamma)`, write `X = X_L` for the plane sextic and identify `|L| = (P^2)^vee`. Let\\n`I := {(x,l) in C x (P^2)^vee : phi_L(x) in l}`\\nwith projections `pr_C` and `rho: I -> (P^2)^vee`.\\n\\n**Lemma 6.** `I` is a `P^1`-bundle over `C`, hence smooth and irreducible of dimension two, and `rho` is finite flat of degree six. Its branch locus is a curve, and `|L|^{rs} = (P^2)^vee \\\\ Branch(rho)`.\\n\\n*Proof.* For fixed `x`, the lines through `phi_L(x)` form a line in `(P^2)^vee`; the incidence is the projectivization of a rank-two quotient bundle, hence a Zariski-locally trivial `P^1`-bundle over `C`. So `I` is smooth irreducible of dimension two. `rho` is proper with fibres `rho^{-1}(l) = supp(phi_L^*l)`, a finite set, so `rho` is finite; a finite morphism from a Cohen-Macaulay surface to a regular surface with equidimensional fibres is flat (miracle flatness), of degree `deg L = 6`. A point `l` lies in `|L|^{rs}` iff `phi_L^*l` is reduced, i.e. iff `rho` is unramified over `l`. QED\\n\\n**Lemma 7 (transitivity).** The monodromy group `G := im(pi_1^{et}(|L|^{rs}, *) -> S_6)` is transitive.\\n\\n*Proof.* `I` is irreducible and `rho` is finite surjective, so the generic fibre is a single Galois orbit. QED\\n\\n**Lemma 8 (2-transitivity).** `G` is 2-transitive.\\n\\n*Proof.* Let `I_2 := {(x,x',l) : x != x' in C, phi_L(x) in l, phi_L(x') in l}`. Because `phi_L` is birational onto `X`, the set `Sigma := {(x,x') in C x C : x != x', phi_L(x) = phi_L(x')}` is finite (it lies over the finitely many non-normal points of `X`). On the open set `(C x C) \\\\ (Delta u Sigma)`, which is irreducible of dimension two since `C` is irreducible, the line through `phi_L(x)` and `phi_L(x')` is unique, giving an isomorphism onto a locally closed subset `I_2^o` of `I_2` of dimension two. The complement `I_2 \\\\ I_2^o` is a finite union of fibres `{(x,x')} x (line in (P^2)^vee)` indexed by `Sigma`, hence of dimension one. For a general `l` (avoiding the finitely many lines through singular points of `X`, and avoiding `Branch(rho)`), `phi_L^*l` consists of six distinct points with six distinct images, so all `30` ordered pairs of distinct points of `phi_L^*l` lie in `I_2^o`. Thus `I_2^o -> (P^2)^vee` is dominant, generically finite of degree `30`, with irreducible source, so monodromy is transitive on ordered pairs of distinct points; i.e. `G` is 2-transitive. QED\\n\\n**Lemma 9 (a transposition).** `G` contains a transposition.\\n\\n*Proof.* Let `Ram(rho)` be the ramification locus of `rho` in `I`. A point `(x,l)` lies in `Ram(rho)` iff `phi_L^*l` is non-reduced at `x`, i.e. iff `l` contains the image of the first-order neighbourhood of `x`. In characteristic zero the differential `d phi_L` is nonzero on a dense open subset of `C`, so the set `Z_{cusp} := {x : h^0(L - 2x) = 2}` (where every line through `phi_L(x)` works) is finite. Off `Z_{cusp}` the line is unique and equals the tangent line `T_x`. Hence `Ram(rho)_{red} = sigma(C) u (finitely many fibres of pr_C)` with `sigma: C -> I`, `x -> (x, T_x)`, so `sigma(C)` is an irreducible curve in `I` and `rho(sigma(C)) = X^vee`, the dual curve. `X^vee` is one-dimensional (if it were a point, all tangent lines would coincide and `X` would be a line) and is not a pencil of lines through a point `P_0` (such a pencil is a line in `(P^2)^vee`, and its dual is the point `P_0`, whereas `(X^vee)^vee = X` is a curve by biduality).\\n\\nNow take a general point `x` of `C` and `l := T_x`. By generality:\\n- `x` is not in `Z_{cusp}` and `phi_L` is injective near `x`, so `phi_L(x)` is a smooth point of `X`;\\n- the contact order of `T_x` with `X` at `x` is exactly two, i.e. `h^0(L - 3x) = 0`: the inflection divisor of the base-point-free `g^2_6` given by `L` is an honest effective divisor (the Wronskian is not identically zero in characteristic zero) of degree `(r+1)d + r(r+1)(g-1) = 3*6 + 2*3*5 = 48`, hence has finite support;\\n- `T_x` is not tangent to `X` at any second point: in characteristic zero the Gauss map `C -> X^vee`, `x -> T_x`, is birational onto its image (reflexivity/biduality for reduced irreducible plane curves), so the general tangent line is tangent at exactly one point;\\n- `T_x` avoids the finitely many singular points of `X` and the finitely many points of `phi_L(Z_{cusp})` (each imposes the condition of lying in a fixed line of `(P^2)^vee`, and `X^vee` is contained in no such line);\\n- `X^vee` is smooth at `T_x` (a general point of an irreducible curve).\\n\\nThen `phi_L^*T_x = 2x + x_3 + ... + x_6` with `x, x_3, ..., x_6` pairwise distinct, `rho` has ramification index exactly two at `(x,T_x)` and is unramified at the other four preimages, and the branch curve is smooth at `T_x`. Etale-locally at `(x,T_x)` the map `rho` is therefore `z -> z^2` in one sheet and an isomorphism on each of the other four. A small loop in `|L|^{rs}` around `X^vee` at `T_x` therefore has monodromy the transposition exchanging the two points of `phi_L^*` near `x` and fixing the other four. QED\\n\\n**Proposition 10 (Part (C)).** In type (i), `G = S_6`, and `Ord(|L|^{rs})` is connected.\\n\\n*Proof.* By Lemma 8, `G` is 2-transitive, hence primitive. By Lemma 9 it contains a transposition. By Jordan's theorem a primitive subgroup of `S_n` containing a transposition is all of `S_n`. So `G = S_6`. Connectedness of the torsor `Ord(|L|^{rs})` is equivalent to transitivity of `G` on the six points, which holds a fortiori. QED\\n\\nThe torsor `Ord(|L|^{rs}) -> |L|^{rs}` is the fibrewise instance of the finite etale degree-720 `S_6`-torsor of [V4] (ATT-0088/CLM-0088-5), and `|L|^{rs}` is a nonempty open subset of the irreducible `|L| ~= P^2` by [V3] and [V4], hence connected, so `pi_1^{et}(|L|^{rs})` and its image `G` are well defined up to conjugacy.\\n\\n## 6. Part (D): imprimitivity in type (ii)\\n\\n**Proposition 11.** In type (ii), `G` is contained in a conjugate of `S_2 wr S_3` (order 48); in particular `G` is imprimitive and `G != S_6`.\\n\\n*Proof.* Here `phi_L = j o psi` with `j: Y -> P^2` the embedding of the smooth plane cubic and `psi: C -> Y` the double cover. For a line `l`, `phi_L^*l = psi^*(j^*l)`. If `l` is in `|L|^{rs}` then `phi_L^*l` is reduced with six distinct points, which forces `j^*l = y_1+y_2+y_3` with `y_1,y_2,y_3` distinct and none of them a branch point of `psi`; hence `phi_L^*l` is the disjoint union of the three two-element fibres `psi^{-1}(y_1), psi^{-1}(y_2), psi^{-1}(y_3)`. The morphism `psi` is defined on all of `C`, independently of `l`, so this partition into three blocks of size two is preserved by the monodromy action along any loop in `|L|^{rs}`. Therefore `G` preserves a nontrivial block system and is contained in the corresponding wreath product `S_2 wr S_3`, of order `2^3 * 6 = 48 < 720`. QED\\n\\n## 7. Part (E): balanced bielliptic-composed covers exist\\n\\n**Proposition 12.** Let `Y/k` be an elliptic curve, `P` in `Pic^2(Y)`, `q := phi_{|P|}: Y -> P^1` the associated degree-two morphism (with four ramification points), and `M` in `Pic^5(Y)`. Let `y_0` be the unique point of `Y` with `O_Y(y_0) ~= M (x) P^{-2}` (unique because `deg(M - 2P) = 1` and a degree-one line bundle on an elliptic curve has `h^0 = 1`). Assume `y_0` is not a ramification point of `q`, and let `B` in `|M^{(x)2}|` be reduced. Let `psi: C -> Y` be the double cover with `psi_*O_C = O_Y (+) M^{-1}` branched along `B`, and `gamma := q o psi`. Then `C` is smooth connected of genus six, `gamma` is finite flat of degree four, and, granting (CE) and (N) for `(C,gamma)`, hypothesis (BAL) holds: `F ~= O(4) (+) O(5)`.\\n\\n*Proof.* *Step 0: the cover.* `B` reduced makes `C` smooth; `M^{-1} != O_Y` makes `C` connected; Riemann-Hurwitz `2g(C) - 2 = 2(2*1-2) + deg B = 10` gives `g(C) = 6`. `gamma` is a composition of finite flat morphisms of degrees `2` and `2`. Such data exist: `Pic^5(Y) -> Y`, `M -> y_0`, is a bijection (translation of the Abel-Jacobi isomorphism), so only four values of `M` are excluded; and `M^{(x)2}` has degree `10 >= 3`, hence is very ample on `Y`, so a general member of `|M^{(x)2}|` is reduced (Bertini, characteristic zero).\\n\\nLet `iota` be the deck involution of `psi`; all sheaves below carry their canonical `iota`-linearizations and superscripts `+`, `-` denote the eigen-parts, which are direct summands because `2` is invertible.\\n\\n*Step 1: pushforwards.* `psi_*O_C = O_Y[+] (+) M^{-1}[-]`. By relative duality `psi_*omega_C = Hom(psi_*O_C, omega_Y) = omega_Y[+] (+) (omega_Y (x) M)[-] = O_Y[+] (+) M[-]`; the eigen-labels are correct because the trace pairing is `iota`-invariant and pairs `+` with `+` and `-` with `-`. Also `omega_C = O_C(Ram)` and the canonical section of `O_C(2 Ram) = psi^*O_Y(B)` is `iota`-invariant, so `omega_C^{(x)2} ~= psi^*(M^{(x)2})` **equivariantly with trivial twist**, whence\\n`psi_*(omega_C^{(x)2}) = M^{(x)2} (x) psi_*O_C = M^{(x)2}[+] (+) M[-]`.\\nAs line bundles, `omega_C = psi^*M`, `D = gamma^*O(1) = psi^*P`, and `L = omega_C(-D) = psi^*(M - P)`.\\n\\n*Step 2: `E = O(2)[+] (+) O(3)[-] (+) O(4)[-]`.* `q_*O_Y = O (+) O(-2)` (degree `-(g(Y)+1) = -2` and `h^0 = 1`). For `q_*M` (rank two, degree `5 - 1 - 1 = 3`): `h^0(q_*M(-1)) = h^0(M-P) = 3` and `h^0(q_*M(-2)) = h^0(M - 2P) = 1`; among `O(a)+O(b)` with `a+b = 3` only `(a,b) = (1,2)` gives `(0)_+ +(3)_+ ... ` matching both, i.e. `3 = 1+2` and `1 = 0+1`. So `q_*M = O(1) (+) O(2)`. Hence\\n`gamma_*omega_C = q_*(psi_*omega_C) = (O (+) O(-2))[+] (+) (O(1) (+) O(2))[-]`.\\nOn the other hand `gamma_*omega_C = omega_{P^1} (+) E (x) omega_{P^1} = O(-2) (+) E(-2)`, and the summand `omega_{P^1}` is `+` (the natural map `gamma^*omega_{P^1} -> omega_C` is `iota`-equivariant with trivial action on the source). Matching degrees, `E(-2) = O(0)[+] (+) O(1)[-] (+) O(2)[-]`, i.e. `E = O(2)[+] (+) O(3)[-] (+) O(4)[-]`. (This is consistent with [V3]: every summand has degree at least two.)\\n\\n*Step 3: section levels.* For every `m`,\\n`H^0(C, omega_C (x) gamma^*O(m))^+ = H^0(Y, mP)`, `H^0(C, omega_C (x) gamma^*O(m))^- = H^0(Y, M + mP)`,\\n`H^0(C, omega_C^{(x)2} (x) gamma^*O(m))^+ = H^0(Y, M^{(x)2} + mP)`, `(...)^- = H^0(Y, M + mP)`.\\nWith Lemma 1(3) the generators are\\n`xi_2` in `H^0(omega_C)^+ = H^0(Y, O_Y)` (dimension one), corresponding to `1`;\\n`xi_3` in `H^0(omega_C (x) gamma^*O(-1))^- = H^0(Y, M-P)` (dimension three), call it `nu`;\\n`xi_4` in `H^0(omega_C (x) gamma^*O(-2))^- = H^0(Y, M-2P)` (dimension one), call it `mu`, with `div(mu) = y_0`.\\nUnder the splitting `q_*M = O(1) (+) O(2)`, `H^0(M-P) = H^0(q_*M(-1)) = <nu> (+) q^*H^0(O_{P^1}(1)) * mu`, where `nu` spans the `O(0)`-part. Every element of the second summand vanishes at `y_0`; since `deg(M-P) = 3 >= 3` makes `M-P` very ample on `Y`, hence base-point-free, we conclude `nu(y_0) != 0`.\\nProducts: the multiplications `omega_Y (x) M -> M` and `omega_Y (x) omega_Y -> M^{(x)2}` are, respectively, the identity and multiplication by the section `beta` of `M^{(x)2}` cutting `B` (because `div(xi_2) = Ram` and `2 Ram = psi^*B`). Hence `xi_3^2 -> nu^2`, `xi_3 xi_4 -> nu mu`, `xi_4^2 -> mu^2`, `xi_2 xi_4 -> mu`, `xi_2 xi_3 -> nu`, `xi_2^2 -> beta`.\\n\\n*Step 4: the two eigen-components of `res`.* The monomials with `a_i + a_j >= 6` and their coefficient spaces are: `x_2x_4` (coefficients `H^0(O(0))`, parity `-`), `x_3^2` (`H^0(O(0))`, `+`), `x_3x_4` (`H^0(O(1))`, `+`), `x_4^2` (`H^0(O(2))`, `+`); the monomials `x_2^2` and `x_2x_3` have coefficient spaces `H^0(O(-2)) = 0` and `H^0(O(-1)) = 0`. So `h^0(Sym^2E(-6)) = 1 + 1 + 2 + 3 = 7`, with a one-dimensional `-` part and a six-dimensional `+` part. The target `H^0(C, L^{(x)2}) = H^0(C, omega_C^{(x)2} (x) gamma^*O(-2))` has `+` part `H^0(Y, M^{(x)2} - 2P)` of dimension `10 - 4 = 6` and `-` part `H^0(Y, M - 2P)` of dimension one, total seven, matching 0.2. Since `res` is `iota`-equivariant and `char k = 0`, `ker(res) = ker(res^+) (+) ker(res^-)`.\\n\\n*The `-` component* sends the generator `x_2x_4` to (a nonzero multiple of) `mu != 0`, hence is injective.\\n\\n*The `+` component* is\\n`Phi: k (+) H^0(O_{P^1}(1)) (+) H^0(O_{P^1}(2)) -> H^0(Y, M^{(x)2} - 2P)`,\\n`(a, c_1, c_2) -> a nu^2 + q^*(c_1) nu mu + q^*(c_2) mu^2`,\\nboth sides of dimension six. Suppose `Phi(a,c_1,c_2) = 0`. Put `f := nu/mu`, a nonzero rational section of `P` with `div(f) = D_nu - y_0`, where `D_nu := div(nu) >= 0` has degree three and `y_0` is not in `supp(D_nu)` (Step 3). So `f` has a pole of order exactly one at `y_0` and no other pole.\\n\\n- (`alpha`) **`f` is not `iota`-invariant.** If it were, `div(f)` would be `iota`-invariant; its polar part is exactly `-y_0` (coefficient `-1` at `y_0`, `>= 0` elsewhere), and the polar part of `iota^*div(f)` is exactly `-iota(y_0)`, so `y_0 = iota(y_0)`, i.e. `y_0` is a ramification point of `q` -- excluded by hypothesis.\\n- (`beta`) **`a = 0` is impossible.** Then `mu (q^*(c_1) nu + q^*(c_2) mu) = 0`, and `Y` is integral with `mu != 0`, so `q^*(c_1) nu + q^*(c_2) mu = 0`. If `c_1 = 0` then `c_2 = 0`; otherwise `f = -q^*(c_2/c_1)` is `iota`-invariant, contradicting (`alpha`).\\n- (`gamma`) **`a != 0` is impossible.** Choose `c` in `H^0(O_{P^1}(1))` nonzero with zero `z_c != q(y_0)`, and put `tau := q^*c`, a section of `P` with `div(tau) = q^{-1}(z_c)`, which contains neither `y_0` nor `iota(y_0)`. Write `f = F tau` with `F` in `k(Y)`. Dividing the relation by `a tau^2` gives\\n  `F^2 + q^*(c_1/(a c)) F + q^*(c_2/(a c^2)) = 0`,\\n  a monic quadratic for `F` over the index-two subfield `q^*k(P^1)` of `k(Y)`. By (`alpha`), `F` is not in `q^*k(P^1)`, so this is the minimal polynomial of `F` and therefore `q^*(c_1/(ac)) = -(F + iota^*F) = -Tr(F)`.\\n  Now `ord_{y_0}(F) = -1` exactly (`y_0` is in neither `D_nu` nor `q^{-1}(z_c)`), while `iota^*F` is regular at `y_0` (its poles lie in `{iota(y_0)} u q^{-1}(z_c)`, which does not contain `y_0`). Hence `ord_{y_0}(Tr(F)) = -1`. Since `q` is unramified at `y_0`, `Tr(F) = q^*(g)` with `ord_{q(y_0)}(g) = -1`. But `g = -c_1/(ac)` with `c_1` in `H^0(O(1))` regular and `c(q(y_0)) != 0`, so `g` is regular at `q(y_0)`, a contradiction.\\n\\nHence `Phi` is injective, `res` is injective, and by Lemma 1(4) `h^0(F(-6)) = 0`, i.e. `F ~= O(4) (+) O(5)`. QED\\n\\nBy SRC-IN-1, `(CE)` and `(N)` hold for every such `(C, gamma)`: `gamma` is a finite flat Gorenstein cover of degree four of `P^1`, so Casnati-Ekedahl 1996 Theorem 2.1 supplies the relative-canonical embedding and the two-term resolution, and SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6 supplies `O_{P(E^vee)}(1)|_C ~= omega_C (x) gamma^*O(2)`. This is Part (E). Note that Proposition 12 does **not** invoke any criterion of ATT-0100 (in particular not its (2.1) or (3.3)); balancedness is derived here from Lemma 1(4) directly, which is what FND-0150 requires of any attempt asserting balancedness for a concrete example.\\n\\n*Consequences.* (1) Frontier obligation \\\"whether a non-primitive degree-four pencil factoring through a positive-genus intermediate curve can have balanced CE splitting\\\" is answered **affirmatively for intermediate genus one**. (2) In particular the extended working-context constraint that constancy of `h^0(omega_C(-D)) = 3` on the balanced locus is *not* equivalent to excluding all non-primitive degree-four pencils is now realized by actual balanced examples, not merely left open. (3) Primitivity of `gamma` is therefore **not** available as a property of the balanced locus, and any downstream argument that needs `phi_L` birational must impose type (i) explicitly.\\n\\n## 8. Part (F): the family statement\\n\\nLet `S` be a reduced locally Noetherian `k`-scheme carrying a genus-six degree-four family with (CE), (BAL), (N) on every geometric fibre, as in ATT-0088 Part III. By [V5] (ATT-0088/CLM-0088-8) `AJ = P_S(pi_*L)` is a Zariski-locally trivial `P^2`-bundle, `U` (the reduced-support open) is `S`-smooth of pure relative dimension two and surjective onto `S`, and `Z = Ord(U) -> U` is a finite etale right `S_6`-torsor whose restriction to the fibre over a geometric point `b` of `S` is exactly the fibrewise torsor `Ord(|L_b|^{rs}) -> |L_b|^{rs}` of [V4].\\n\\n**Proposition 13.** Let `S_alpha` be a connected component of `S` and suppose some geometric point `b` of `S_alpha` has type (i). Then `H_alpha := im(pi_1^{et}(U_alpha, *) -> S_6) = S_6`, and `Z_alpha` is connected. If moreover `U_alpha` is normal, integral and locally Noetherian, then `Z_alpha` is irreducible, `[S_6 : H_alpha] = 1`, and the induction functor `Ind_{H_alpha}^{S_6}` of FND-0152 is the identity functor.\\n\\n*Proof.* `U_b = |L_b|^{rs}` is a locally closed subscheme of `U_alpha` and the restriction of `Z_alpha` to it is the fibrewise ordering torsor. Functoriality of the etale fundamental group gives a commutative triangle in which the image of `pi_1^{et}(U_b) -> S_6` (which is the fibrewise monodromy group, equal to `S_6` by Proposition 10) is contained in the image of `pi_1^{et}(U_alpha) -> S_6`. Hence `H_alpha = S_6`. A finite etale `G`-torsor over a connected base has connected components indexed by the orbit set `H \\\\ G` of left multiplication by the monodromy image `H` (packet FND-0148, FND-0151); with `H_alpha = S_6` there is exactly one orbit, so `Z_alpha` is connected. Under the additional hypothesis that `U_alpha` is normal, integral and locally Noetherian, connected components of a finite etale cover coincide with irreducible components (this normality hypothesis is exactly the one FND-0132 and FND-0148 insist on and it is retained here, not dropped), so `Z_alpha` is irreducible with `[S_6 : H_alpha] = 1`. The last clause is immediate from FND-0152 with `H_alpha = S_6`. QED\\n\\nProposition 13 is deliberately stated component-locally and needs **no** irreducibility hypothesis on the balanced base: the monodromy conclusion is obtained from a single fibre, so it is insensitive to the open frontier obligation on irreducibility of the balanced base, and it does not infer irreducibility of any splitting stratum from irreducibility of the classical Hurwitz space.\\n\\n## 9. Separation of inputs, and what is not claimed\\n\\n**Proved here from scratch:** Lemma 1 (balancedness criterion), Lemma 2 (plane quintics are unbalanced, including the classification of `g^1_4`'s on a plane quintic), Lemma 3 (`L != T^{(x)2}`), Proposition 4 (Part (A)), Proposition 5 (Part (B)), Lemmas 6-9 and Proposition 10 (Part (C)), Proposition 11 (Part (D)), Proposition 12 (Part (E), modulo (CE)+(N)), Proposition 13 (Part (F)).\\n\\n**Verified dependency inputs used:** [V1]-[V7] above, all dual-confirmed, cited by artifact and claim identifier.\\n\\n**Source-backed inputs used:** SRC-0004 (Canning-Larson Lemma 10.6, the CE degree formula and the relative-canonical restriction) and Casnati-Ekedahl 1996 Theorem 2.1, packaged as SRC-IN-1 and used only in Part (E) to discharge (CE)+(N) for the constructed cover, exactly as FND-0150 prescribes. Two classical facts are used in Lemma 9 and are flagged as such: characteristic-zero reflexivity/biduality for plane curves (so the Gauss map is birational onto the dual curve), and Jordan's theorem that a primitive permutation group containing a transposition is symmetric.\\n\\n**Computational hypotheses:** none. No finite experiment is used anywhere; in particular the existence statement (E) is proved for every admissible `(Y,P,M,B)` rather than verified on one model, and the hyperelliptic input [V2] is used in the model-free form recorded by REV-0135 and REV-0138.\\n\\n**Not claimed** (see also `theorem_scope.not_covered`): the exact monodromy image in type (ii) (only containment in `S_2 wr S_3`); whether a connected component of the balanced base can be entirely of type (ii), i.e. whether the type-(i) hypothesis in Part (F) is automatic; non-primitive pencils through intermediate curves of genus two or three; any statement about `Aut(C,Gamma)` or `Aut(C,gamma,Gamma)`, moduli inertia, or stack-level full faithfulness; any morphism to `M_{6,6}` and its faithfulness, quasi-finiteness, closedness, image closure or absolute codimension; closedness, purity, lci or Gysin statements for the pair `(M_{6,6},Z)`; nonreduced bases and algebraic-stack bases (the FND-0127 smooth-atlas descent is not carried out here); unbalanced CE strata; integral coefficients and coarse-space statements; and, emphatically, any Hodge, weight, purity or Tate conclusion. In particular Part (C) is a statement about a geometric monodromy image only: by FND-0053, FND-0085 and FND-0152(ii) it upgrades nothing to Tate type, and the `H_alpha`-object `F(Z_alpha^1)` of FND-0152 remains a separate geometric and Hodge input. What Part (C) does supply downstream is that for type-(i) components the induction in FND-0152 is trivial, so `F(Z_alpha) = F(U_alpha)`-computations need no coset bookkeeping.\\n\\n## 10. Consistency checks against the recorded corpus\\n\\n- `E = O(2)+O(3)+O(4)` in the plane-quintic case reproduces the working-context established facts `Sym^2E(-6) ~= O(-2)+O(-1)+O(0)^2+O(1)+O(2)` with `h^0 = 7`, and `h^0(2H+2p) = 7` with the six-dimensional subspace `H^0(2H)`; the recorded base-point statement `L = H+p` with `p` a base point is exactly Step 4 of Lemma 2.\\n- The bielliptic-composed case has `h^0(O_C(D)) = 2` and `h^0(L) = 3`, consistent with [V3], [V6] and FND-0142, and `C` is not hyperelliptic (Castelnuovo-Severi: a degree-two map to `P^1` and a degree-two map to `Y` would need a common degree-two factor, forcing `P^1 ~= Y`), consistent with [V2].\\n- `E = O(3)^{(+)3}` occurs both in the excluded trigonal case and in the balanced `(1,2)`-`(2,2)` complete intersection in `P^1 x P^2` recorded as a reusable computation; there is no conflict, since `F` is not determined by `E`, and the trigonal exclusion turns on `H^0(L)` being a pullback along a degree-three map. The complete-intersection example is type (i) and is not used as an input.\\n- Poincare-duality bookkeeping for the campaign target (`d = 3*6-3+6 = 21`, `2d-16 = 26`, `Q(-13)(21) = Q(8)`) is untouched by this lemma, which makes no cohomological claim.\", \"claims\": [{\"id\": \"CLM-0103-1\", \"statement\": \"Assume (CE) and (N). With gamma_*O_C = O + (+)_i O(-a_i), E = (+)_i O(a_i), sum a_i = 9, one has F = p_*(I_C(2)), an exact sequence 0 -> F -> Sym^2E -> gamma_*R -> 0 with F of rank two and degree nine, and ker(H^0(Sym^2E(-6)) -> H^0(C, L^{tensor 2})) = H^0(F(-6)). Writing F = O(s)+O(9-s) with s <= 4, h^0(F(-6)) = 4-s; hence (BAL) holds if and only if that restriction map is injective. The target H^0(C, L^{tensor 2}) always has dimension seven.\", \"status\": \"proved\"}, {\"id\": \"CLM-0103-2\", \"statement\": \"On a smooth plane quintic of genus six every g^1_4 is |H - p_0| for a point p_0; consequently L = H + p_0 has p_0 as a base point, E = O(2)+O(3)+O(4), and the restriction map of CLM-0103-1 has seven-dimensional source but image inside the six-dimensional H^0(omega_C), so h^0(F(-6)) >= 1. Hence no degree-four pencil on a smooth plane quintic has balanced CE relations bundle.\", \"status\": \"proved\"}, {\"id\": \"CLM-0103-3\", \"statement\": \"Under (CE)+(N)+(BAL), L is base-point-free. Equivalently, the fixed part of |L| left open by ATT-0088/CLM-0088-4 (degree zero or one) has degree zero.\", \"status\": \"proved\"}, {\"id\": \"CLM-0103-4\", \"statement\": \"Under (CE)+(N)+(BAL), L is not isomorphic to T^{tensor 2} for any base-point-free degree-three pencil T. The proof shows h^0(4T - omega_C) = 0 (otherwise D = omega_C - 2T acquires a base point, contradicting D = gamma^*O(1)), whence E = O(3)^3, the restriction map is the multiplication Sym^2 H^0(L) -> H^0(L^{tensor 2}) whose image lies in the five-dimensional t^*H^0(O_{P^1}(4)), and h^0(F(-6)) >= 1.\", \"status\": \"proved\"}, {\"id\": \"CLM-0103-5\", \"statement\": \"Under (CE)+(N)+(BAL), phi_L : C -> P^2 is finite onto an irreducible nondegenerate plane curve and exactly one of the following holds: deg phi_L = 1 with image a plane sextic of geometric genus six (type (i)); or deg phi_L = 2 with image a smooth plane cubic Y, phi_L = psi a bielliptic double cover, omega_C = psi^*M with deg M = 5, L = psi^*N with deg N = 3, gamma = q o psi with q = phi_{|M tensor N^{-1}|} of degree two, and E = O(2)+O(3)+O(4) (type (ii)). Degrees 3 and 6, singular cubic images, hyperelliptic C and smooth plane quintic C do not occur.\", \"status\": \"proved\"}, {\"id\": \"CLM-0103-6\", \"statement\": \"In type (i), the geometric monodromy image of pi_1^{et}(|L|^{rs}) -> S_6 acting on the six points of a general divisor of |L| is the full symmetric group S_6, and the finite etale S_6-torsor Ord(|L|^{rs}) is connected. The proof uses irreducibility of the point-line incidence (transitivity), irreducibility of the ordered-pair incidence (2-transitivity, using birationality of phi_L), a simple tangent line at a general point of the dual curve (a transposition, using characteristic-zero reflexivity and finiteness of the inflection divisor), and Jordan's theorem.\", \"status\": \"proved\"}, {\"id\": \"CLM-0103-7\", \"statement\": \"In type (ii), the fibres of psi induce a monodromy-invariant partition of each reduced divisor of |L| into three blocks of size two; hence the fibrewise geometric monodromy image is contained in the imprimitive wreath subgroup S_2 wr S_3 of order 48 and is not S_6.\", \"status\": \"proved\"}, {\"id\": \"CLM-0103-8\", \"statement\": \"Type (ii) is nonempty: for every elliptic Y, every P in Pic^2(Y) with associated degree-two q, every M in Pic^5(Y) whose unique point y_0 with O(y_0) = M tensor P^{-2} avoids the four ramification points of q, and every reduced B in |M^{tensor 2}|, the double cover psi : C -> Y branched along B is a smooth connected genus-six curve and gamma = q o psi is a finite flat degree-four cover satisfying (BAL). Hence a non-primitive degree-four pencil factoring through an intermediate curve of genus one can have balanced Casnati-Ekedahl splitting, and primitivity of gamma is not a property of the balanced locus.\", \"status\": \"proved\"}, {\"id\": \"CLM-0103-9\", \"statement\": \"Over a reduced locally Noetherian k-scheme S carrying a balanced (CE)+(N) genus-six degree-four family, with U the reduced-support open of AJ = P_S(pi_*L) and Z = Ord(U) the S_6-torsor of ATT-0088/CLM-0088-8: for every connected component S_alpha over which at least one geometric fibre is of type (i), the geometric monodromy image H_alpha equals S_6 and Z_alpha is connected; if moreover U_alpha is normal, integral and locally Noetherian then Z_alpha is irreducible, [S_6 : H_alpha] = 1, and the FND-0152 induction functor is the identity. No irreducibility hypothesis on S or on the balanced base is used.\", \"status\": \"proved\"}], \"proof_dependencies\": [\"ATT-0088\", \"REV-0139\", \"REV-0142\", \"ATT-0086\", \"REV-0135\", \"REV-0138\", \"ATT-0066\", \"REV-0110\", \"REV-0116\", \"ATT-0065\", \"REV-0108\", \"REV-0109\", \"SRC-0004\", \"FND-0105\", \"FND-0125\", \"FND-0132\", \"FND-0139\", \"FND-0142\", \"FND-0148\", \"FND-0150\", \"FND-0151\", \"FND-0152\"], \"experiment_ids\": [], \"experiment_uses\": [], \"novelty_claims\": [{\"id\": \"NOV-0103-1\", \"statement\": \"Base-point-freeness of L on the balanced locus is proved. ATT-0088/CLM-0088-4 established only that the fixed part of |L| has degree at most one, and REV-0142 records explicitly that no claim is made that L itself is base-point-free; ATT-0088 theorem_scope lists this as neither asserted nor denied. The missing ingredient supplied here is the classification of g^1_4's on a smooth plane quintic together with an explicit vanishing-order computation excluding balancedness there.\"}, {\"id\": \"NOV-0103-2\", \"statement\": \"A complete dichotomy for the degree-six morphism phi_L on the balanced locus: it is either birational onto a plane sextic or exactly two-to-one onto a smooth plane cubic with gamma bielliptic-composed. This includes a new exclusion of the trigonal possibility L = T^{tensor 2}, obtained without the Maroni/Hirzebruch classification of trigonal curves.\"}, {\"id\": \"NOV-0103-3\", \"statement\": \"First determination of a geometric monodromy image for the finite etale S_6-cover of the C66-GEO-COMP frontier: the fibrewise monodromy is the full S_6 in type (i). This addresses the recorded frontier obligation that neither fullness of S_6, nor the existence of a transposition, nor the occurrence loci were established; both fullness and an explicit transposition (local monodromy at a general point of the dual sextic) are now proved, and the occurrence locus is identified as exactly the type-(i) locus.\"}, {\"id\": \"NOV-0103-4\", \"statement\": \"Affirmative resolution, for intermediate genus one, of the frontier obligation on whether a non-primitive degree-four pencil factoring through a positive-genus intermediate curve can have balanced CE splitting. The construction is universal in (Y,P,M,B) rather than a single model, and its balancedness proof uses only the Lemma-1 criterion, not any criterion of ATT-0100, so it complies with FND-0150.\"}, {\"id\": \"NOV-0103-5\", \"statement\": \"A component-local family statement (Part (F)) that yields H_alpha = S_6 from a single geometric fibre and therefore requires no irreducibility hypothesis on the balanced base, sidestepping the still-open frontier obligation on base irreducibility.\"}], \"gap_markers\": [{\"id\": \"GAP-0103-1\", \"severity\": \"medium\", \"statement\": \"The exact monodromy image in type (ii) is not determined; only containment in S_2 wr S_3 is proved. Determining whether it equals the full wreath product (and hence computing the isotypic decomposition on the type-(ii) locus) is left open.\", \"affects\": \"Part (D)\"}, {\"id\": \"GAP-0103-2\", \"severity\": \"medium\", \"statement\": \"It is not determined whether a connected component of the balanced base can consist entirely of type-(ii) fibres, i.e. whether the type-(i) hypothesis in Part (F) is automatic. This is a question about the dimension and closure of the bielliptic-composed sublocus inside the balanced base, and it interacts with the still-open frontier obligation on irreducibility of that base. Part (F) is stated conditionally on the hypothesis and does not assume it away.\", \"affects\": \"Part (F)\"}, {\"id\": \"GAP-0103-3\", \"severity\": \"medium\", \"statement\": \"Non-primitive degree-four pencils factoring through intermediate curves of genus two or three are not analyzed. Only intermediate genus zero (excluded, via ATT-0088/CLM-0088-2 and FND-0125) and intermediate genus one (realized, Part (E)) are settled. Note that in the dichotomy of Part (B) such covers, if balanced, would have to be of type (i), since their phi_L cannot factor through the intermediate curve by degree reasons; so this gap does not affect (A)-(D) or (F).\", \"affects\": \"completeness of the non-primitivity picture, not the theorem\"}, {\"id\": \"GAP-0103-4\", \"severity\": \"low\", \"statement\": \"Part (F) is stated over reduced locally Noetherian schemes, matching ATT-0088 Part III. The extension to algebraic stacks with a smooth atlas (the one-line descent recorded as FND-0127) and to nonreduced bases (where Grauert fails, FND-0131) is not carried out here.\", \"affects\": \"Part (F) base scope\"}, {\"id\": \"GAP-0103-5\", \"severity\": \"low\", \"statement\": \"Hypotheses (CE) and (N) are carried as explicit hypotheses in Parts (A)-(D) and (F) and are discharged in Part (E) only by the source-backed input SRC-IN-1 (Casnati-Ekedahl 1996 Theorem 2.1 and SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6). No independent construction of the CE embedding or resolution is given here.\", \"affects\": \"Part (E) and the standing hypotheses\"}, {\"id\": \"GAP-0103-6\", \"severity\": \"low\", \"statement\": \"Lemma 9 invokes two classical characteristic-zero inputs that are not reproved: reflexivity/biduality for reduced irreducible plane curves (birationality of the Gauss map onto the dual curve) and Jordan's theorem on primitive groups containing a transposition.\", \"affects\": \"Part (C)\"}], \"failed_approaches_addressed\": [{\"kind\": \"blocked_route\", \"subject\": \"vcd-only-vanishing\", \"disposition\": \"Not used. No cohomological degree, virtual cohomological dimension, or vanishing-above-vcd argument appears anywhere in this artifact.\"}, {\"kind\": \"blocked_route\", \"subject\": \"undecorated-cgp-top-weight-graph-complex\", \"disposition\": \"Not used. No tropical moduli, graph complex, or top-weight computation appears; the artifact makes no weight-graded claim at all.\"}, {\"kind\": \"blocked_route\", \"subject\": \"point-count-without-degree-parity-separation\", \"disposition\": \"Not used. There is no point count and no finite experiment; every existence statement in Part (E) is proved for all admissible parameter data, and the exclusions in Lemmas 2 and 3 are universal section-count arguments.\"}, {\"kind\": \"blocked_route\", \"subject\": \"forgetful-map-renaming-local-system-unknown\", \"disposition\": \"Not used. The forgetful map M_{6,6} -> M_{6,5} and its Leray local system do not occur; no local system is renamed or claimed to be understood.\"}, {\"kind\": \"working_context_constraint\", \"subject\": \"Concluding pure-Tate cohomology of the failure locus from rationality of the irreducible representations of S_6, or from a finite branch morphism of the Hurwitz stack (FND-0053, FND-0085)\", \"disposition\": \"Explicitly avoided. Section 9 states that the S_6 monodromy result upgrades nothing to Tate type, and points to FND-0152(ii): the H_alpha-object attached to a component remains a separate geometric and Hodge input.\"}, {\"kind\": \"working_context_constraint\", \"subject\": \"Upgrading the count of pi_1^etale-orbits to a count of irreducible components without a normality hypothesis on the base (FND-0132, FND-0144)\", \"disposition\": \"Avoided. Proposition 13 concludes connectedness unconditionally and irreducibility only under the explicit hypothesis that U_alpha is normal, integral and locally Noetherian; the stack-inertia caveat of FND-0144 is respected by stating Part (F) over schemes.\"}, {\"kind\": \"working_context_constraint\", \"subject\": \"Proving a stabilizer or inertia clause by observing that an automorphism induces a PGL_2 transformation of P^1 fixing the marked images (FND-0137)\", \"disposition\": \"Avoided. No stabilizer, inertia, or automorphism-group statement of any kind is made; the corresponding frontier obligation is left untouched and is listed in theorem_scope.not_covered.\"}, {\"kind\": \"working_context_constraint\", \"subject\": \"Inferring irreducibility of the balanced open H_3 directly from irreducibility of the classical Hurwitz space\", \"disposition\": \"Avoided. The monodromy conclusion in Part (F) is derived from a single geometric fibre and is component-local, so no irreducibility or density property of the balanced base or of any splitting stratum is used or asserted.\"}, {\"kind\": \"working_context_constraint\", \"subject\": \"Deducing a global direct-sum splitting W = W_4 + W_5 from fibrewise balancedness (FND-0097)\", \"disposition\": \"Avoided. All of Parts (A)-(E) are statements about a single geometric fibre, and Part (F) uses only the fibrewise hypotheses plus the verified ATT-0088 Part III conclusions; no global splitting of W over a base is used.\"}, {\"kind\": \"working_context_constraint\", \"subject\": \"Declaring p_*L and R^1p_*L to be vector bundles from constancy of fibre dimensions over a possibly nonreduced base (FND-0131)\", \"disposition\": \"Avoided. Part (F) assumes S reduced, exactly the scope of ATT-0088 Part III and ATT-0086.\"}, {\"kind\": \"working_context_constraint\", \"subject\": \"Grounding a claim asserted for every degree-two map q : P^1 -> P^1 on the single coordinate model z = x^2\", \"disposition\": \"Avoided. The type-(ii) analysis and the existence proof in Part (E) are carried out for arbitrary (Y, P, M, B); no coordinate model is used, and the hyperelliptic input is taken in the model-free form re-derived by REV-0135 and REV-0138.\"}, {\"kind\": \"working_context_constraint\", \"subject\": \"Deriving a local complete intersection or regular-immersion conclusion from expected-versus-actual determinantal codimension (FND-0068)\", \"disposition\": \"Not used. No lci, regularity, depth or conormal claim is made here; the regular-immersion statement of ATT-0088/CLM-0088-9 is neither used nor extended.\"}, {\"kind\": \"working_context_constraint\", \"subject\": \"Treating the balanced reduced-support relative linear system as a closed pure-codimension-four subvariety of M_{6,6} and applying a Gysin morphism (FND-0052, FND-0091, FND-0098)\", \"disposition\": \"Avoided. No morphism to M_{6,6} is constructed or used, no closedness, purity, codimension or Gysin statement is made, and no monomorphism into an evaluation-failure locus in M_{6,6} is asserted.\"}, {\"kind\": \"working_context_constraint\", \"subject\": \"Concluding non-closedness of the balanced piece from openness of balancedness plus one balanced example\", \"disposition\": \"Avoided. Part (E) exhibits balanced examples but draws no closure or non-closedness conclusion from them.\"}, {\"kind\": \"working_context_constraint\", \"subject\": \"Positing a balanced component decomposition containing a nonempty jump piece B_4 with fibre P^3 (FND-0139)\", \"disposition\": \"Avoided. h^0(L) = 3 identically on the balanced locus is taken from the confirmed [V3] and [V6]; no P^3 piece is posited.\"}, {\"kind\": \"adjudicated_finding_discipline\", \"subject\": \"FND-0150: asserting (CE) and (N) for a concrete cover without construction or citation, and using ATT-0100's criterion (3.3) or splitting identification (2.1) to conclude balancedness\", \"disposition\": \"Complied with. Part (E) discharges (CE)+(N) for the constructed cover by citing Casnati-Ekedahl 1996 Theorem 2.1 and SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6 (packaged as SRC-IN-1), which is the route FND-0150 sanctions, and proves balancedness from the independently established criterion of Lemma 1(4) rather than from any ATT-0100 statement.\"}, {\"kind\": \"adjudicated_finding_discipline\", \"subject\": \"FND-0101: asserting a clause while recording a high-severity gap marker against it\", \"disposition\": \"Complied with. Every gap marker concerns a statement outside the theorem; the asserted clauses of (A)-(F) are either proved here, explicitly hypothesized, or cited from dual-confirmed artifacts, and the open items are listed in theorem_scope.not_covered rather than asserted with a disclaimer.\"}], \"methods_used\": [\"casnati-ekedahl-quadric-sequence-from-ideal-resolution\", \"projective-bundle-derived-pushforward\", \"relative-duality-tschirnhausen-splitting\", \"twisted-section-count-balancedness-criterion\", \"riemann-roch-and-serre-duality\", \"clifford-theorem-with-equality-cases\", \"base-point-free-pencil-trick\", \"plane-model-degree-and-nondegeneracy-analysis\", \"vanishing-order-at-a-base-point-obstruction\", \"trigonal-pullback-multiplication-rank-bound\", \"double-cover-eigen-decomposition-and-relative-duality\", \"elliptic-curve-norm-trace-minimal-polynomial-pole-argument\", \"bertini-and-branch-divisor-construction\", \"incidence-variety-irreducibility-for-monodromy-transitivity\", \"ordered-pair-incidence-for-two-transitivity\", \"dual-curve-simple-tangency-local-monodromy-transposition\", \"jordan-primitive-group-with-transposition\", \"etale-fundamental-group-functoriality-from-a-fibre\", \"finite-etale-torsor-orbit-component-count-with-normality\"], \"new_inputs\": [], \"completion_attestation\": {\"resolves_exact_target\": false, \"no_undischarged_dependencies\": true, \"not_reduction_only\": true, \"no_problem_status_claim\": true, \"exact_problem_web_search_used\": false}, \"status\": \"claimed_complete\", \"source_claim_ids\": [\"FND-0105\", \"FND-0125\", \"FND-0132\", \"FND-0139\", \"FND-0142\", \"FND-0148\", \"FND-0150\", \"FND-0151\", \"FND-0152\"], \"source_ids\": [\"SRC-0004\"], \"engine\": \"claude\"}","ttft_ms":348874,"ttft_stream_ms":10070,"time_to_req